#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# minimal_by

#% text
# `minimal_by(xs, f)` gives every element of `xs` with the smallest `f(x)`, in their order; `minimal_by(xs, f, n)` the `n` elements with the smallest keys, smallest first.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For an association, the entries with the smallest values.

#% item
# `n` may be `up_to(n)` (at most `n`); ties keep their order.

#% item
# `minimal_by(f)(xs)` is `minimal_by(xs, f)`.

#% item
# `min_by` gives the first such element alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
minimal_by([[a, 1], [b, 1], [a, 2], [d, 1], [b, 3]], last)
#% output
#= [[a, 1], [b, 1], [d, 1]]

#% cell
minimal_by([[a, 1], [b, 1], [b, 3]], first, up_to(4))
#% output
#= [[a, 1], [b, 1], [b, 3]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `maximal_by`, `min_by`, `sort_by`
